WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.250
- [Narrator] This Great
Big Story was made possible

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.026
by Shell, make the future.

00:00:06.026 --> 00:00:07.800
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.700
- [Megan Leftwich] As an engineer,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.330
I'm always watching how things swim

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.410
and sort of thinking about them.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.000
The goal is to really
be inspired by nature,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.170
and take the system
that nature has created

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.520
and understand the physics,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.593
and then build that
capability into a new vehicle.

00:00:27.078 --> 00:00:29.560
(upbeat music)

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.740
I'm Megan Leftwich,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.540
associate professor of
mechanical engineering

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.400
at the George Washington University,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.633
and I study sea lions.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.900
Most animals swim with
their tails or their fluke

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.550
or whatever's the back of their body.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.260
But sea lions use their fore-flippers

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.900
and clap them into their
body to generate a thrust

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.490
that can propel them through
the water at incredible speeds.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.090
This form of swimming is found

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.373
in almost no other swimming animal.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.700
If we could understand how sea lions move,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.540
we could design vehicles
that could one day

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.260
stealthy explore
shipwrecks, underwater mines

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.463
or unexplored caves.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.700
In 2013, I started to get
serious about studying sea lions,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.640
and I put together a team of
students and other researchers

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.840
and keepers at the
Smithsonian National Zoo.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.040
So I thought, well, we could
use our modern techniques

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.250
and really get into the
nitty-gritty of the physics

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.640
of how these animals swim.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.810
And so, we 3D-print
three pieces with joints

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.070
that represent the elbow
joint and the wrist joint.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:32.090
And we use different silicone gels

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.153
to create a soft, robotic flipper.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.700
And then what we're
able to do is use motors

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.400
to actually actuate that.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.890
So, we can recreate
that motion that we got

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.340
from filming the animals at the zoo

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.940
directly from the animals.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.760
We, then, put this onto
this robotic platform

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.440
to study, not only, how the animals move

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.680
but how the animals move the water.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.900
And so, understanding the physics of that

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.470
can allow us to then build
some of those capabilities

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.273
into the engineered vehicles that we have.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.540
It's important to take notes from nature

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.760
when inventing technology

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.120
because nature has already
done so much of the work us.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.410
And so, by looking around,

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.410
we can definitely broaden our horizons

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.930
and see solutions that already exist.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.100
We're taking nature, which
seems to solve problems

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.100
so elegantly and being inspired
by those elegant solutions.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.180
And you know, we often
say like, nature does that

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.910
so much better than us and then move on.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.530
But maybe if we say,
well how do they do it?

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.833
We could start to increase
our own functionality.

00:02:35.118 --> 00:02:37.701
(upbeat music)

